  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA surface high pressure nearly stalled over the \nOhio Valley and an upper level ridge building in from the west \ncontinues to bring fair weather today. This trend will continue \nthrough this weekend as upper-level heights increase and the \nsurface high moves east into the Mid-Atlantic.\n\nAbove average temperatures make a return today across eastern \nNew York and western New England, with highs remaining in the \n40s and 50s. High temperatures will gradually climb through the \nend of this week, up to the 50s and 60s by Thursday which will \nremain through the weekend. Partly cloudy skies are also \nexpected to stick around through Thursday, mainly across our \nnorthern areas, enhanced by persistent westerly flow and \nmoisture advection from the Great Lakes. Areas in and around the\nMid-Hudson Valley will not be as impacted by these clouds, \nleading to greater cooling during the overnight periods when \ncompared to regions north of the Capital District.\n\nCurrent model guidance continues to show a weak shortwave trough \nbriefly moving across our northern areas on Thursday night into \nFriday. However, no precipitation is expected due to a lack of \nmoisture. The next chance of precipitation appears to arrive \nSunday night with a frontal passage.",
